#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""kupdate - rolling Talos Linux upgrade, one release at a time, with live checks.

Upgrades every node of a Talos cluster through the latest patch of each
intermediate minor release (e.g. 1.8.0 -> 1.8.4 -> 1.9.6 -> ...), one node at a
time: control planes first, then workers. Before, during and after every node
it checks that the cluster and the published endpoints keep working, and it
stops at the first problem.

Usage:
  kupdate ENV --plan            read-only: checks, upgrade path, disruption report
  kupdate ENV [options]         run the upgrade (asks to type ENV to confirm)
  kupdate ENV --certs [--plan]  only renew (or show) the admin client certificates
                                of talosconfig and kubeconfig; nothing on the cluster changes

ENV selects the configs (patterns overridable with environment variables):
  KUPDATE_KUBECONFIG   default ~/.kube/config-{env}       (or --kubeconfig FILE)
  KUPDATE_TALOSCONFIG  default ~/TalosLinux/{env}/talosconfig  (or --talosconfig FILE)
The starting version is read from the nodes, the path (latest patch of every
release line) and the Kubernetes compatibility from GitHub and the official
Talos support matrix: it works for any Talos version, no version is hard-coded.
Neither ~/.kube/config nor $TALOSCONFIG are used or modified.

Stops (exit 1) when:
  - a node is not Ready, etcd is not healthy, a Longhorn volume is degraded/faulted,
    a Deployment/StatefulSet/DaemonSet that was healthy at start is not ready
    again within the timeouts, `talosctl health` fails;
  - an endpoint that answered at start fails --fail-after checks in a row
    (connection error, timeout or HTTP 5xx);
  - draining a node would stop every ready replica of a workload (a single
    replica, or all replicas on that node): that would be downtime. Use
    --allow-disruption to accept it (endpoints must then recover in time);
  - the next Talos release does not support the running Kubernetes version
    (use --with-k8s to upgrade Kubernetes one minor at a time when needed);
  - a node was cordoned outside kupdate, or an upgraded node lost a system
    extension, its schematic or an active extraKernelArgs entry.
Endpoints (Ingress hosts plus the --endpoints file) are probed every --interval
seconds, 4 at a time, without following redirects (3xx counts as an answer).
They should resolve to internal addresses: do not monitor URLs behind a WAF or
a rate-limited proxy.
A node upgrade already started is never interrupted: on an error, Ctrl-C,
SIGTERM or SIGHUP kupdate waits for it to finish, then stops. Run it inside
tmux or screen. Nodes cordoned by kupdate carry the annotation
kupdate/cordoned=true and the next run uncordons them once Ready; nodes at or
above the target version are skipped, so after fixing the cause you can simply
run kupdate again.
"""

